      "id": 1,
      "text": "Shaniko was originally called Cross Hollow when a post office was established there in May 1879.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaniko%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Known as the Wool Capital of the World, Shaniko once served as a massive economic hub for the region's wool industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/shaniko-oregon-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Columbia Southern Railway turned Shaniko into a bustling terminal in 1900, driving single-day wool sales exceeding one million dollars.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/shaniko-oregon-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Historic structures remaining in Shaniko include the 1902 Columbia Southern Hotel, a jail, and a schoolhouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/shaniko-oregon-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Residents of Shaniko keep the town's history alive through annual events like the Ragtime and Vintage Music Festival.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        23,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
